        'Look at any drawn triangle: its lines are slightly crooked, its angles imperfect. Yet geometers prove theorems about the perfect triangle no one has ever seen. Plato generalized this insight into a complete metaphysics: behind every imperfect, changing thing in our world stands a perfect, eternal Form — of Beauty, Justice, Equality, even of the bed a carpenter builds. The sensible world "participates" in these Forms the way a photograph participates in its subject: derivatively, imperfectly. Knowledge — as opposed to opinion — is always knowledge of Forms, grasped by reason rather than the senses. The theory explains how mathematics works, why moral standards bind everyone, and what we mean when we say two beautiful things share "the same" beauty.',
        'Prisoners chained since childhood in a cave watch shadows cast on a wall and take them for reality. One prisoner is freed, dragged painfully up into the sunlight, and gradually sees real things, then the sun itself. Returning to warn the others, he is mocked — they would kill him if they could. The allegory, told in the Republic, compresses Plato’s whole philosophy of education: learning is not pouring knowledge into an empty mind but turning the soul around, away from appearances toward what is. It is also political autobiography — Athens had executed Socrates, the freed prisoner who returned. Every educator who has fought comfortable illusion since has been retelling this story.',
