        'Plato was born around 428 BC to one of the noblest families of Athens, with ancestors among the lawgivers and a future, everyone assumed, in politics. Two catastrophes redirected him: the moral collapse of the city during the Peloponnesian War, and the execution of his teacher Socrates in 399 BC by the restored democracy. Disgusted with every existing regime, he concluded that nothing short of a refounding of politics on philosophy could save mankind — and that he must first work out what philosophy could know.',
        'After years of travel — Megara, Egypt, Cyrene, and the Pythagorean communities of southern Italy, where mathematics ruled both science and mysticism — he returned and founded the Academy around 387 BC, in a grove sacred to the hero Academus. It was the first enduring institution of higher learning in the West: geometry over the door, dialectic within, and a research community that included Eudoxus the astronomer, Theaetetus the mathematician, and for twenty years a brilliant student from the north named Aristotle. The Academy would last, in various forms, over 900 years — until Justinian closed the pagan schools in 529 AD.',
